export const O1={title:'The profession of occupational therapy',hook:'Occupational therapy helps people do the things they need and want to do—getting dressed, cooking, going to school, working, playing and caring for others. Those everyday activities are called occupations, and restoring them after illness, injury or disability is the heart of the profession.',objectives:['Describe the Occupational Therapy event, its test plan and Round Two skills.','Define occupation and the domain of occupational therapy.','Compare the roles, education and credentials of occupational therapists and assistants.','Describe the history, settings and outlook of the profession.'],sections:[
['The Occupational Therapy event',[
'Round One is a **50-question test in 60 minutes** with no time announcements at ILC. The test plan: **profession of OT 10%**, **models of practice 10%**, **activity analysis and occupation-based activities 10%**, **OT for physical conditions 20%**, **OT for wellness and mental health 20%**, **OT across the lifespan—children, adults and older adults 20%** and **ethical and safety considerations 10%**. Questions come from O’Brien and Conners’ *Introduction to Occupational Therapy* and Atchison and Dirette’s *Conditions in Occupational Therapy*, with Hall’s *Occupational Therapy Toolkit* used for skills. The event is sponsored by **Why Choose OT?**',
'Round Two is a written-scenario skills assessment; each skill has **5 minutes**: **I** instruct a patient to put on a shirt with one hand, **II** use a sock aid, **III** transfer from floor to standing, **IV** transfer from standing to floor, **V** take heart rate before and after activity, **VI** stretch break exercises, **VII** demonstrate good body mechanics and **VIII** proper sitting posture. Most skills are **instruction** skills—you coach the patient through each step—so clear, encouraging communication earns points. A combined Round Two score of **70% or higher** is required for recognition; final rank adds test and skill scores, with ties broken by the test score.',
match('Match the skill to its focus',[['Skill I','Dressing with one hand'],['Skill II','Using a sock aid'],['Skills III and IV','Getting up from and down to the floor'],['Skill V','Monitoring heart rate with activity'],['Skill VI','Stretch breaks'],['Skills VII and VIII','Body mechanics and sitting posture']])]],
['What is occupation?',[
'In occupational therapy, **occupation** means the everyday activities that give life meaning and purpose—not just jobs. The **Occupational Therapy Practice Framework (OTPF)**, published by the American Occupational Therapy Association (AOTA), groups occupations as **activities of daily living (ADLs)**—bathing, dressing, eating, toileting, functional mobility and personal hygiene; **instrumental activities of daily living (IADLs)**—cooking, shopping, managing money and medications, driving and caring for others; **health management**; **rest and sleep**; **education**; **work**; **play**; **leisure**; and **social participation**. OT also considers **client factors** (values, body functions and structures), **performance skills** (motor, process and social interaction), **performance patterns** (habits, routines, roles and rituals) and **contexts** (environmental and personal factors).',
sort('ADL or IADL?',['ADL (basic self-care)','IADL (more complex community and home tasks)'],[['Dressing',0],['Bathing',0],['Feeding oneself',0],['Toileting',0],['Preparing a meal',1],['Managing medications',1],['Grocery shopping',1],['Driving',1]])]],
['OT practitioners and education',[
'**Occupational therapists (OTs)** evaluate clients, set goals, plan and carry out intervention and measure outcomes. Entry to the profession requires a **master’s (MOT/MS) or entry-level doctoral (OTD)** degree from an accredited program, fieldwork, passing the **NBCOT** certification exam (earning **OTR**, Occupational Therapist, Registered) and state licensure (OTR/L). **Occupational therapy assistants (OTAs)** complete an **associate degree** (or a bachelor’s program), fieldwork and the NBCOT exam (**COTA**, Certified Occupational Therapy Assistant), then state licensure; they carry out intervention under the supervision of an OT and contribute to evaluation and documentation. **Occupational therapy aides** perform support tasks such as preparing equipment and are not licensed practitioners. The Bureau of Labor Statistics projects faster-than-average growth for both OTs and OTAs as the population ages.',
match('Match the practitioner to the description',[['Occupational therapist (OTR/L)','Master’s or doctorate; evaluates and plans intervention'],['OT assistant (COTA/L)','Associate degree; delivers intervention under OT supervision'],['OT aide','Support tasks; not a licensed practitioner'],['NBCOT','National certification exam organization'],['AOTA','National professional association'],['Fieldwork','Supervised clinical training in school']])]],
['History of the profession',[
'Occupational therapy grew from the **moral treatment** movement of the 1800s, which used meaningful activity and humane care for people with mental illness, and from the **Arts and Crafts movement**. In **1917**, the **National Society for the Promotion of Occupational Therapy** was founded by **George Barton**, **William Rush Dunton Jr.**, **Eleanor Clarke Slagle**, **Susan Cox Johnson**, **Thomas Kidner** and **Isabel Newton**; it later became the **American Occupational Therapy Association (AOTA)**. **Eleanor Clarke Slagle** developed **habit training** for people with mental illness and is often called the mother of occupational therapy. Rehabilitation of injured soldiers in **World Wars I and II** expanded the profession into physical disabilities, and later decades added pediatrics (with federal education laws), school-based practice and community wellness.',
flip('People and milestones',[['1917','National Society for the Promotion of Occupational Therapy founded'],['Eleanor Clarke Slagle','Habit training; often called the mother of OT'],['William Rush Dunton Jr.','Physician and founder; promoted occupation in psychiatry'],['George Barton','Architect and founder; coined “occupational therapy”'],['Moral treatment','1800s humane care using meaningful activity'],['World Wars','Rehabilitation of soldiers expanded physical disabilities practice']])]],
['Where OT practitioners work',[
'OT practitioners work across the lifespan and the health system: **hospitals** (acute care and intensive care), **inpatient rehabilitation**, **skilled nursing facilities**, **outpatient clinics** (including hand therapy), **home health**, **schools** (helping students participate in education), **early intervention** programs for infants and toddlers, **mental health** programs, **community** and wellness programs, **workplaces** (ergonomics and return to work), **driver rehabilitation** and **hospice**. The goal in every setting is the same: enable participation in meaningful occupation. OT practitioners collaborate with physical therapists, speech-language pathologists, nurses, physicians, teachers, social workers, families and clients.',
chk('Which question best reflects occupational therapy’s focus?',['What does the client need and want to do in daily life?','Which bone is fractured?','What medication should be prescribed?','How fast can the client run a mile?'],'OT centers on participation in meaningful occupations.')]],
['OT compared with related professions',[
'OT and **physical therapy (PT)** often work together, but their emphasis differs: PT focuses on **movement, strength, balance and mobility**, while OT focuses on **participation in daily occupations**, using movement, cognition, perception, emotion and the environment to make tasks possible. For example, after a hip replacement, PT may focus on walking and stair climbing, while OT teaches the client to dress with hip precautions, bathe safely with a tub bench and return to cooking. **Speech-language pathologists** address communication and swallowing; **recreational therapists** use leisure; **rehabilitation counselors** support employment. Understanding these differences helps on the test and in teamwork.',
kase('Which profession?',{a:{text:'After a stroke, a 64-year-old cannot button his shirt, struggles to cook and wants to return to fishing. Which professional focuses on these goals?',choices:[['Occupational therapist','b','Correct. These are daily occupations—ADLs, IADLs and leisure.',true],['Radiologic technologist','b','Radiology does not address daily occupations.',false]]},b:{text:'He also has difficulty walking long distances and climbing stairs. Who leads that goal?',choices:[['Physical therapist, working alongside OT','c','Correct. PT focuses on mobility and gait; OT and PT collaborate.',true],['Nobody—it is not important','c','Mobility supports participation and is addressed by PT.',false]]},c:{text:'The team coordinates: OT works on dressing, cooking and adapted fishing; PT works on walking and stairs.',end:true}})]],
["Becoming an OT practitioner",["Students preparing for occupational therapy take biology, anatomy and physiology, psychology, sociology and statistics, and many programs ask for **observation hours** with OT practitioners in different settings. Graduate programs accredited by the **Accreditation Council for Occupational Therapy Education (ACOTE)** include classroom learning and two levels of **fieldwork**: **Level I** fieldwork introduces students to practice through short observations and participation, and **Level II** fieldwork is extended, full-time supervised practice (at least 24 weeks for OT students and 16 weeks for OTA students) where students build entry-level competence. Doctoral programs add a capstone experience.","After graduation, practitioners pass the **NBCOT** exam, obtain a state license and maintain their credentials through **continuing education** and continuing competence. Many pursue advanced or specialty credentials, such as the **Certified Hand Therapist (CHT)**, AOTA board and specialty certifications in areas like pediatrics, gerontology, mental health, low vision and driving, or the **Assistive Technology Professional (ATP)** credential. Strong OT practitioners combine scientific knowledge with creativity, patience, problem-solving and the ability to motivate people through difficult recoveries.",{"t":"check","q":"Which fieldwork level involves extended, full-time supervised practice before graduation?","options":["Level II fieldwork","Level I fieldwork","Observation hours only","Continuing education"],"why":"Level II fieldwork builds entry-level competence."}]]
],takeaways:['Test plan: profession 10%, models 10%, activity analysis 10%, physical conditions 20%, wellness and mental health 20%, lifespan 20%, ethics and safety 10%; eight 5-minute instruction skills.','Occupations include ADLs, IADLs, health management, rest and sleep, education, work, play, leisure and social participation (OTPF).','OTs hold a master’s or OTD and the OTR; OTAs hold an associate degree and the COTA; AOTA began in 1917 with founders including Eleanor Clarke Slagle.'],terms:[['Occupation','Everyday activities that bring meaning and purpose.'],['ADL','Basic self-care such as bathing, dressing and eating.'],['IADL','Complex tasks such as cooking, shopping and managing money.']],mcqs:[q('In occupational therapy, “occupation” refers to:','Meaningful everyday activities','Only paid employment','Only exercise','Only hobbies','Occupation includes ADLs, IADLs, work, play and more.'),q('Which is an instrumental activity of daily living?','Managing medications','Bathing','Toileting','Feeding oneself','IADLs are more complex tasks.'),q('The entry-level degree for an occupational therapist is:','A master’s or entry-level doctorate','An associate degree','A high school diploma','A certificate','OTAs complete an associate degree.'),q('Which organization administers the national certification exam?','NBCOT','AOTA','HOSA','CDC','Passing earns the OTR or COTA.'),q('Who is often called the mother of occupational therapy?','Eleanor Clarke Slagle','Florence Nightingale','Clara Barton','Marie Curie','She developed habit training.'),q('The organization that became AOTA was founded in:','1917','1776','1965','2001','It began as the National Society for the Promotion of OT.'),q('Who must supervise an occupational therapy assistant?','An occupational therapist','A physical therapy aide','A nurse aide','No one','OTAs work under OT supervision.'),q('How long is each Round Two skill?','5 minutes','15 minutes','30 seconds','20 minutes','All eight skills are 5 minutes.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain what occupational therapy is, how it differs from physical therapy, and the roles and education of occupational therapists and occupational therapy assistants.',rubric:[['Definition and occupations',['occupation','daily','adl','iadl','participation','meaningful']],['Comparison with PT',['physical therapy','movement','mobility','participation','collaborat']],['Practitioner roles and education',['master','doctor','otr','associate','cota','supervision']],['Settings or history',['school','hospital','rehabilitation','1917','slagle','community']]],model:'Occupational therapy enables people of all ages to participate in occupations—the meaningful everyday activities they need and want to do, including ADLs such as dressing and bathing, IADLs such as cooking and managing medications, education, work, play, leisure, rest and social participation. While physical therapy focuses mainly on movement, strength, balance and mobility, OT focuses on performing daily occupations, adapting tasks, teaching new methods and modifying environments; the two professions often collaborate, for example PT on walking and OT on dressing and bathing after a hip replacement. Occupational therapists complete a master’s or entry-level doctoral degree with fieldwork, pass the NBCOT exam to become an OTR and obtain a state license; they evaluate, plan and carry out intervention. Occupational therapy assistants complete an associate degree and fieldwork, pass the NBCOT exam to become a COTA and are licensed; they deliver intervention under an OT’s supervision. OT practitioners work in hospitals, rehabilitation, schools, early intervention, mental health, home health and community settings, continuing a tradition that began with the 1917 founding of what became AOTA.'},sources:[RULES,AOTA,NBCOT,BLS_OT,BLS_OTA]};

export const O2={title:'Models of practice and the OT process',hook:'Why does one therapist start with a client’s routines and values while another starts with range of motion? The answer is models of practice and frames of reference—organized ways of thinking that guide evaluation and intervention. They are 10% of the test and shape how you explain every skill.',objectives:['Describe the OT process of evaluation, intervention and outcomes.','Explain major occupation-based models: PEO, MOHO and CMOP-E.','Compare frames of reference such as biomechanical, rehabilitative, sensory integration and cognitive-behavioral.','Choose intervention approaches: create, establish/restore, maintain, modify and prevent.'],sections:[
['The OT process',[
'The OT process has three parts. **Evaluation** begins with the **occupational profile**—the client’s history, interests, values, routines, roles and goals—followed by an **analysis of occupational performance**, observing the client doing tasks and using standardized assessments to identify what supports or limits performance. **Intervention** includes the plan, implementation and review: the OT sets measurable goals with the client, selects approaches and activities, delivers or delegates intervention and adjusts it. **Outcomes** measure whether the client achieved goals such as independence, participation, quality of life and well-being. The process is **client-centered** and collaborative: the client’s priorities drive goals.',
order('Arrange the OT process',['Receive the referral','Gather the occupational profile','Analyze occupational performance','Set goals with the client','Plan and implement intervention','Review and adjust intervention','Measure outcomes and discharge'])]],
['Occupation-based models',[
'**Models of practice** explain how people, occupations and environments interact. The **Person-Environment-Occupation (PEO) model** shows occupational performance as the overlap of the person, the environment and the occupation; improving the fit among the three improves performance. The **Model of Human Occupation (MOHO)**, developed by Gary Kielhofner, describes **volition** (motivation, values and interests), **habituation** (habits and roles) and **performance capacity** (physical and mental abilities) interacting with the environment. The **Canadian Model of Occupational Performance and Engagement (CMOP-E)** places spirituality at the center of the person, surrounded by occupations (self-care, productivity, leisure) and environment, and is used with the **Canadian Occupational Performance Measure (COPM)**, in which clients rate their own performance and satisfaction. The **OTPF** describes the domain and process of practice in the United States.',
match('Match the model to its key idea',[['PEO model','Performance comes from the fit of person, environment and occupation'],['MOHO','Volition, habituation and performance capacity'],['CMOP-E','Spirituality at the core; client rates performance with the COPM'],['OTPF','AOTA’s description of OT’s domain and process'],['Client-centered practice','The client’s priorities set the goals'],['Occupational profile','History, interests, routines and goals of the client']])]],
['Frames of reference',[
'**Frames of reference** guide specific interventions. The **biomechanical** frame addresses range of motion, strength and endurance—for example, exercises after a hand fracture. The **rehabilitative (compensatory)** frame teaches new methods, adaptive equipment and environmental changes when function cannot be restored—for example, a sock aid or one-handed dressing. **Motor control and neurodevelopmental** approaches address movement after neurological injury such as stroke. **Sensory integration**, developed by **A. Jean Ayres**, addresses how children process sensory input to support behavior and learning. **Cognitive-behavioral** approaches change thoughts and behaviors in mental health practice, and **developmental** frames follow typical skill progression in children. Therapists often combine frames.',
sort('Biomechanical or rehabilitative (compensatory)?',['Biomechanical (restore capacity)','Rehabilitative (compensate and adapt)'],[['Range-of-motion exercises for a stiff wrist',0],['Strengthening grip with putty',0],['Teaching one-handed shirt dressing',1],['Using a sock aid after hip surgery',1],['Building endurance with graded activity',0],['Installing a tub bench',1]])]],
['Intervention approaches',[
'The OTPF names five approaches. **Create or promote** (health promotion) supports performance for everyone without assuming disability—for example, a stretch-break program for office workers. **Establish or restore** (remediation) builds skills that are not yet developed or restores impaired ones—handwriting practice or strengthening. **Maintain** preserves abilities so they do not decline—a routine for a person with a progressive condition. **Modify** (compensation, adaptation) changes the task or environment—adaptive equipment, energy conservation or home modifications. **Prevent** reduces the risk of problems—fall prevention, ergonomic training and joint protection. Many HOSA skills fit these approaches: stretch breaks and body mechanics (promote and prevent), sock aid and one-handed dressing (modify) and floor transfers (prevent and modify).',
match('Match the example to the approach',[['Workplace stretch-break program','Create or promote'],['Practicing handwriting to build skill','Establish or restore'],['Routine to keep a person with MS independent','Maintain'],['Sock aid after hip replacement','Modify (compensate)'],['Teaching safe floor transfers to prevent injury','Prevent'],['Grab bars in the bathroom','Modify (adapt the environment)']])]],
['Therapeutic use of self and clinical reasoning',[
'**Therapeutic use of self** is the practitioner’s planned use of personality, insights and communication to build rapport and motivate the client. **Clinical reasoning** is how practitioners think through care: **scientific** reasoning about the condition, **narrative** reasoning about the client’s story, **pragmatic** reasoning about resources and setting, **ethical** reasoning about right action and **interactive** reasoning about the relationship. Evidence-based practice combines research, clinical expertise and client values. In Round Two, therapeutic use of self appears in “used appropriate verbal and nonverbal communication”—greeting warmly, explaining clearly, encouraging and checking understanding.',
chk('A therapist adjusts the plan because the client cares more about returning to gardening than to bowling. Which type of reasoning is this?',['Narrative reasoning about the client’s story and values','Scientific reasoning about pathology','Pragmatic reasoning about reimbursement','Procedural reasoning about diagnosis'],'Narrative reasoning centers the client’s story and meaning.')]],
['Applying a model',[
kase('Choosing a frame',{a:{text:'A 70-year-old with severe arthritis cannot put on socks because her hips do not bend far enough, and her goal is to dress independently. Which frame fits first?',choices:[['Rehabilitative—teach adaptive equipment such as a sock aid','b','Correct. Compensation enables the task now.',true],['Sensory integration','b','Sensory integration targets sensory processing, usually in children.',false]]},b:{text:'Using the PEO model, what else could improve her performance?',choices:[['Change the environment—sit in a stable chair with armrests and keep clothes within reach','c','Correct. Improving the fit of environment and occupation supports performance.',true],['Nothing; only her body matters','c','PEO emphasizes person, environment and occupation together.',false]]},c:{text:'With a sock aid, a stable chair and clothes organized within reach, she dresses independently. The outcome is documented.',end:true}})]],
["Assessments and evidence-based practice",["OT evaluation combines **nonstandardized** methods—interviews, observation of tasks, checklists—with **standardized assessments** that have set procedures and scoring, allowing comparison over time or with norms. Examples include the **Canadian Occupational Performance Measure (COPM)** for client-identified goals; the **Barthel Index** for basic ADLs; the **Assessment of Motor and Process Skills (AMPS)** for task performance; the **Allen Cognitive Level Screen** for cognitive functioning; the **Beery Developmental Test of Visual-Motor Integration** for children’s visual-motor skills; and measures of client factors such as **goniometry** (joint range of motion), **manual muscle testing**, **dynamometer** grip strength and sensory testing. Levels of assistance—**independent, modified independent** (uses a device), **supervision**, **minimal, moderate or maximal assistance** and **dependent**—describe how much help a client needs.","**Evidence-based practice** integrates the best available research, the practitioner’s clinical expertise and the client’s values and circumstances. Practitioners ask focused clinical questions, search and appraise research, apply findings and measure outcomes. Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials provide strong evidence, but they are always weighed with the client’s goals and context.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the assessment to what it measures","pairs":[["COPM","Client-rated performance and satisfaction"],["Goniometer","Joint range of motion"],["Dynamometer","Grip strength"],["Barthel Index","Independence in basic ADLs"],["Beery VMI","Visual-motor integration in children"],["Allen Cognitive Level Screen","Cognitive functioning level"]]}]]
],takeaways:['OT process: evaluation (occupational profile and performance analysis), intervention (plan, implement, review) and outcomes.','Models: PEO (fit of person, environment, occupation), MOHO (volition, habituation, performance capacity), CMOP-E with COPM.','Frames: biomechanical, rehabilitative (compensatory), motor control, sensory integration (Ayres), cognitive-behavioral; approaches: create, establish/restore, maintain, modify, prevent.'],terms:[['Frame of reference','A theory-based guide for specific interventions.'],['Volition','Motivation, values and interests in MOHO.'],['Compensatory approach','Changing the task or environment when function cannot be restored.']],mcqs:[q('In MOHO, habits and roles are part of:','Habituation','Volition','Performance capacity','Environment','Volition is motivation.'),q('The PEO model explains performance as the fit among:','Person, environment and occupation','Bones, muscles and nerves','Doctor, nurse and therapist','Diet, exercise and sleep','Improving fit improves performance.'),q('Teaching a client to use a sock aid reflects which frame?','Rehabilitative (compensatory)','Biomechanical','Sensory integration','Developmental','It compensates for limited hip motion.'),q('Who developed sensory integration theory?','A. Jean Ayres','Gary Kielhofner','Eleanor Clarke Slagle','William Dunton','It addresses sensory processing.'),q('A workplace stretch program for all employees is which approach?','Create or promote','Restore','Maintain','Modify','It promotes health without assuming disability.'),q('The first part of evaluation is the:','Occupational profile','Discharge summary','Billing code','Home program','It captures history, values and goals.'),q('The Canadian Occupational Performance Measure asks clients to:','Rate their own performance and satisfaction','Run on a treadmill','Take a written exam','Draw a clock','It is client-centered.'),q('Range-of-motion and strengthening exercises reflect which frame?','Biomechanical','Rehabilitative','Cognitive-behavioral','Psychodynamic','It restores capacity.')],frq:{prompt:'A client recovering from a hand fracture wants to return to cooking. Explain how you would use the OT process, a model of practice and at least two frames of reference or intervention approaches to plan care.',rubric:[['OT process',['evaluation','occupational profile','goal','intervention','outcome']],['Model of practice',['peo','moho','cmop','person','environment','occupation','volition']],['Frames or approaches',['biomechanical','rehabilitative','compensat','restore','modify','prevent']],['Client-centered application',['cooking','client','value','adaptive','grip','kitchen']]],model:'I would begin evaluation with an occupational profile, learning why cooking matters to the client, her routines, roles and goals, then analyze her performance while preparing a simple meal and measure grip strength, range of motion and pain. Using the PEO model, I would look at the fit among the person (hand strength and motion), the environment (kitchen layout, tools) and the occupation (cutting, lifting pots). Together we would set measurable goals, such as preparing a two-dish meal independently. With a biomechanical frame, I would restore capacity through graded range-of-motion and strengthening activities, using therapy putty and functional tasks like stirring. With a rehabilitative frame and the modify approach, I would introduce adaptive equipment—built-up utensil handles, a rocker knife, lightweight pans—and rearrange frequently used items to waist height. I would also prevent re-injury with joint protection techniques. I would review progress, adjust the plan and measure outcomes such as independence and satisfaction in cooking.'},sources:[RULES,AOTA]};

export const O3={title:'Activity analysis, body mechanics and posture (Skills VII and VIII)',hook:'Lifting a laundry basket looks simple until you break it down: balance, grip, trunk control, judgment, timing and a safe path. Activity analysis is OT’s signature skill—seeing every demand a task makes so it can be graded, adapted or taught. Skills VII and VIII apply it to lifting and sitting.',objectives:['Analyze an activity’s demands and the client factors it requires.','Grade and adapt activities to match a client’s abilities and goals.','Demonstrate good body mechanics following HOSA Skill VII.','Instruct proper sitting posture following HOSA Skill VIII.'],sections:[
['What is activity analysis?',[
'**Activity analysis** breaks a task into its parts to understand what it requires. OT practitioners look at **activity demands**: objects and tools used, space and social demands, sequence and timing, required actions and performance skills (motor, process and social), and the body functions and structures involved (strength, range of motion, sensation, cognition, vision). **Occupation-based activity analysis** adds the meaning of the task for a specific person and context—for one client, making coffee is a morning ritual; for another, a way to host friends. The analysis shows which demands a client can meet and which need intervention.',
sort('Classify the demand of making a sandwich',['Motor skill','Process (cognitive) skill'],[['Grasping the knife',0],['Reaching for bread on a shelf',0],['Sequencing the steps in order',1],['Choosing the right tools',1],['Standing balance at the counter',0],['Noticing and fixing a mistake',1]])]],
['Grading and adapting',[
'**Grading** an activity changes its difficulty in small steps so the client is challenged but successful: increase repetitions, weight or resistance, time, complexity, distance or the amount of help, or reduce them when the client struggles. **Adapting** changes the task, tools or environment so it can be done—using a long-handled sponge, sitting instead of standing, using a built-up handle or simplifying steps. The **just-right challenge** keeps tasks motivating. **Purposeful** and **occupation-based** activities—real tasks with meaning to the client—usually produce better engagement and carryover than rote exercise.',
match('Grade or adapt?',[['Increase the weight of the soup can being lifted','Grade (harder)'],['Allow the client to sit while cooking','Adapt'],['Add one more step to a recipe','Grade (harder)'],['Use a jar opener','Adapt'],['Shorten the session when the client tires','Grade (easier)'],['Rearrange items to waist height','Adapt']])]],
['Body mechanics principles',[
'**Body mechanics** is the safe and efficient use of the body during movement. Key principles: keep a **wide base of support**, keep the load **close to the body**, keep the **back straight** with natural curves and lift with the strong **leg muscles** by bending the hips and knees, **avoid twisting**—pivot with the feet—and **push rather than pull**. Plan the lift, test the weight, get help for heavy or awkward loads and use equipment. **Ergonomics** fits the task and workstation to the person to prevent musculoskeletal disorders such as low back pain and tendinitis—both OSHA and NIOSH publish guidance. OT practitioners teach these principles to clients, caregivers and workers.',
flip('Body mechanics rules',[['Base of support','Feet apart; one foot slightly forward'],['Load position','Close to the body, between mid-thigh and mid-chest'],['Spine','Straight with natural curves'],['Power','Lift by straightening the legs'],['Turning','Pivot the feet; never twist the trunk'],['Breathing','Breathe out as you lift']])]],
['Demonstrating good body mechanics (Skill VII)',[
'In Skill VII (5 minutes), the competitor obtains supplies, performs hand hygiene, greets, identifies and explains, then **demonstrates the diagonal lift for medium-weight objects under 25 pounds**: **one foot forward and to the side of the object**, **back straight**, **buttocks pushed out** (hips hinged back), **hands on opposite corners** of the object, **lifting upward by extending the legs** and **breathing out while lifting**. The object is **carried between mid-thigh and mid-chest**; there is **no bending or twisting—the competitor pivots on the feet**; and loads are **not carried on one shoulder, under an arm or in one hand**. The skill ends with hand hygiene and appropriate communication (30 points; 70% is 21).',
order('Diagonal lift (HOSA Skill VII)',['Hand hygiene, greet, identify and explain','Place one foot forward and to the side of the object','Keep the back straight and push the buttocks out','Grasp opposite corners of the object','Lift by extending the legs and breathe out','Carry the object between mid-thigh and mid-chest','Pivot on the feet instead of twisting','Hand hygiene and communicate throughout'])]],
['Proper sitting posture (Skill VIII)',[
'Prolonged poor sitting posture contributes to neck, back and shoulder pain. In Skill VIII (5 minutes), with the patient seated, the competitor obtains supplies, performs hand hygiene, greets, identifies and explains, then **demonstrates that the head is centered directly above the shoulders and hips**; instructs that the **back is straight and supported by the chair**; directs the patient to **place the hips back in the seat**; explains that the **knees are slightly below the hips and slightly extended**; that the **feet are flat on the floor or footrest**; to **relax the shoulders and arms**; to **rest the arms close to the body or on the armrests**; and to **straighten the wrists**. Hand hygiene and communication complete the skill (28 points; 70% is 19.6). At a computer, ergonomic guidance also places the top of the screen at or slightly below eye level and the keyboard at elbow height.',
order('Proper sitting posture (HOSA Skill VIII)',['Hand hygiene, greet, identify and explain','Head centered above the shoulders and hips','Back straight and supported by the chair','Hips back in the seat','Knees slightly below the hips and slightly extended','Feet flat on the floor or footrest','Relax shoulders and arms; arms close to the body or on armrests','Straighten the wrists'])]],
['Applying activity analysis',[
kase('Analyzing a lift',{a:{text:'A grocery worker with back pain lifts 20-pound boxes by bending at the waist and twisting to place them on a shelf. Which change matters most?',choices:[['Teach a diagonal lift with a straight back, leg power and pivoting the feet','b','Correct. Bending at the waist and twisting strain the spine.',true],['Tell him to lift faster','b','Speed increases injury risk.',false]]},b:{text:'The boxes sit on the floor and must go to a high shelf. How could you adapt the task?',choices:[['Raise the boxes onto a cart or platform and use a step stool so the load stays between mid-thigh and mid-chest','c','Correct. Adapting the environment reduces strain.',true],['Have him carry two boxes on one shoulder','c','Loads on one shoulder are unsafe.',false]]},c:{text:'With a cart, a platform and the diagonal lift, his pain decreases and he completes his shift.',end:true}})]],
["Preparatory methods, purposeful activity and occupation",["OT interventions fall along a continuum. **Preparatory methods and tasks** ready the client for occupation: exercise, **orthoses (splints)**, **physical agent modalities** such as heat, cold and electrical stimulation (used by trained practitioners as an adjunct), sensory input and assistive technology setup. **Purposeful activities** are goal-directed tasks that build skills—stacking cones to practice reach, sorting coins to practice fine motor control. **Occupations** are the real, client-chosen activities themselves—cooking breakfast in the home kitchen or dressing for work. Research and the OTPF favor **occupation-based** intervention whenever possible because it motivates clients and transfers directly to daily life, while preparatory methods are used to support it.","**Education and training** for clients and caregivers, **advocacy**, **group interventions** and **virtual interventions** are also OTPF intervention types. In Round Two, every skill is a form of education and training: clear demonstration, step-by-step cues, checking understanding and encouraging independence.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Preparatory, purposeful or occupation?","buckets":["Preparatory method","Purposeful activity","Occupation"],"items":[["Applying a resting hand splint",0],["Warm-up range-of-motion exercises",0],["Stacking cones to practice reaching",1],["Sorting coins for fine motor practice",1],["Cooking breakfast in the client’s kitchen",2],["Getting dressed for work",2]]}]]
],takeaways:['Activity analysis examines objects, space, sequence, actions, performance skills and body functions—plus the meaning of the task for the client.','Grade (change difficulty step by step) and adapt (change task, tools or environment) to reach the just-right challenge.','Skill VII: diagonal lift under 25 lb—foot forward and to the side, back straight, buttocks out, opposite corners, lift with legs, breathe out, carry mid-thigh to mid-chest, pivot; Skill VIII: head over shoulders and hips, back supported, hips back, knees slightly below hips, feet flat, wrists straight.'],terms:[['Activity analysis','Breaking a task into its demands and required skills.'],['Grading','Changing an activity’s difficulty in small steps.'],['Ergonomics','Fitting the task and workplace to the person.']],mcqs:[q('In Skill VII, the diagonal lift is demonstrated for objects under:','25 pounds','5 pounds','50 pounds','100 pounds','Medium-weight objects.'),q('During the diagonal lift, the competitor should:','Breathe out while lifting','Hold their breath','Bend at the waist','Twist to the side','Exhaling reduces strain.'),q('Objects should be carried between:','Mid-thigh and mid-chest','Knees and ankles','Above the head','On one shoulder','Keep loads close and centered.'),q('In Skill VIII, the knees should be:','Slightly below the hips and slightly extended','Higher than the hips','Crossed','Tucked under the chair','Feet flat on the floor.'),q('Changing an activity’s difficulty step by step is called:','Grading','Adapting','Splinting','Charting','It provides the just-right challenge.'),q('Which is a process skill?','Sequencing the steps of a task','Grasping a cup','Reaching overhead','Standing balance','Process skills are cognitive.'),q('When turning with a load, you should:','Pivot your feet','Twist your trunk','Lock your knees','Lean backward','Avoid twisting the spine.'),q('In Skill VIII, the head should be:','Centered directly above the shoulders and hips','Tilted forward toward the screen','Turned to one side','Resting on the hand','Neutral alignment reduces strain.')],frq:{prompt:'Perform an activity analysis of carrying a laundry basket for a client with low back pain, and describe how you would teach the diagonal lift (Skill VII) and good sitting posture (Skill VIII) as part of the plan.',rubric:[['Activity analysis',['demand','grip','balance','sequence','strength','environment']],['Grading or adapting',['grade','adapt','lighter','cart','height','smaller loads']],['Skill VII technique',['foot forward','back straight','buttocks','opposite corners','legs','breathe out','pivot']],['Skill VIII technique',['head','shoulders','hips back','knees','feet flat','wrists','supported']]],model:'Carrying a laundry basket requires bending to the floor, grasping the handles, lifting several pounds, carrying it across rooms and down stairs, balancing, seeing the path and sequencing the task—demands that stress a painful low back. I would adapt the task by having the client use smaller loads, place the basket on a chair or dryer top rather than the floor and use a rolling cart, and grade by increasing load as strength and pain improve. I would teach the diagonal lift: one foot forward and to the side of the basket, back straight, buttocks pushed out, hands on opposite corners, lifting by extending the legs while breathing out, carrying the basket between mid-thigh and mid-chest, pivoting on the feet instead of twisting and never carrying it on one hip or in one hand. Because sitting also affects back pain, I would teach posture: head centered over the shoulders and hips, back straight and supported by the chair, hips back in the seat, knees slightly below the hips and slightly extended, feet flat on the floor, shoulders relaxed, arms close to the body or on armrests and wrists straight.'},sources:[RULES,OSHA_ERGO,NIOSH_LIFT]};

export const O4={title:'Neurological conditions and adaptive dressing (Skills I and II)',hook:'After a stroke, a man whose right arm no longer moves wants to dress himself for work. With the right technique—weak arm in first, strong arm last—and a few tools, he can. Neurological conditions are a large part of the 20% physical conditions domain, and Skills I and II teach exactly these strategies.',objectives:['Describe stroke, traumatic brain injury, spinal cord injury and progressive neurological conditions and their effects on occupation.','Teach one-handed upper-body dressing following HOSA Skill I.','Teach use of a sock aid following HOSA Skill II.','Select adaptive equipment and strategies for ADLs.'],sections:[
['Stroke (cerebrovascular accident)',[
'A **stroke** interrupts blood flow to part of the brain—**ischemic** strokes from blockages and **hemorrhagic** strokes from bleeding. Because each side of the brain controls the opposite side of the body, a stroke often causes **hemiplegia** (paralysis) or **hemiparesis** (weakness) on one side, along with possible **aphasia** (language impairment, usually with left-hemisphere strokes), **dysphagia**, sensory loss, visual field cuts, **unilateral neglect** (ignoring one side of space, usually the left after a right-hemisphere stroke), apraxia and cognitive or emotional changes. OT addresses ADLs and IADLs, upper-extremity function, positioning to prevent shoulder subluxation, safety, cognition and return to roles. Early warning signs follow **BE FAST**.',
sort('Typical with a left- or right-hemisphere stroke?',['Left hemisphere (right-side weakness)','Right hemisphere (left-side weakness)'],[['Aphasia',0],['Right hemiparesis',0],['Left unilateral neglect',1],['Left hemiparesis',1],['Impulsivity and poor safety awareness',1],['Slow, cautious behavior with language deficits',0]])]],
['Traumatic brain injury and spinal cord injury',[
'**Traumatic brain injury (TBI)** results from a blow or jolt to the head; effects range from concussion to severe impairment of cognition, behavior, movement and communication. The **Rancho Los Amigos Levels of Cognitive Functioning** describe recovery from no response through purposeful, appropriate behavior; OT emphasizes safety, structured routines, memory strategies and graded return to activities. **Spinal cord injury (SCI)** causes loss of movement and sensation below the level of injury: **tetraplegia** (quadriplegia) affects all four limbs, **paraplegia** the trunk and legs. Function depends on level—for example, a person with a **C6** injury can use wrist extension to create a **tenodesis grasp**, while a **C5** injury allows elbow flexion and shoulder movement with assistive devices. OT addresses adaptive equipment, wheelchair skills, transfers, skin protection and independence in self-care.',
match('Match the term to its meaning',[['Hemiplegia','Paralysis of one side of the body'],['Unilateral neglect','Ignoring one side of space'],['Aphasia','Impaired language'],['Tetraplegia','Impairment of all four limbs from SCI'],['Tenodesis grasp','Finger closure from wrist extension'],['Rancho Los Amigos levels','Stages of cognitive recovery after TBI']])]],
['Progressive neurological conditions',[
'**Parkinson’s disease** causes tremor, rigidity, slow movement (bradykinesia) and postural instability; OT uses large-amplitude movement strategies, weighted utensils, visual cues and fall prevention. **Multiple sclerosis (MS)** damages myelin, causing fatigue, weakness, sensory and vision problems and heat sensitivity; **energy conservation** and cooling strategies are central. **Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)** progressively weakens muscles; OT focuses on maintaining participation, adaptive equipment, positioning and communication devices. **Guillain-Barré syndrome** causes rapid, often reversible weakness. For progressive conditions, the **maintain** and **modify** approaches dominate, and planning ahead for changing needs is essential.',
chk('Which strategy is especially important for clients with multiple sclerosis?',['Energy conservation and avoiding heat','Maximal strength training to exhaustion','Hot tub sessions before activity','Doing all tasks in the morning without rest'],'Fatigue and heat sensitivity are hallmarks of MS.')]],
['One-handed shirt dressing (Skill I)',[
'The key rule for dressing with one-sided weakness: **dress the weak (affected) side first and undress the strong side first**. In Skill I (5 minutes), after obtaining supplies, hand hygiene, greeting, identifying and explaining, the competitor instructs the patient to **find the sleeve for the weak side** (the right sleeve in the rating sheet’s example); **lean forward and hang the weak arm between the legs**; **move the sleeve up the weak arm with the strong hand**; **move the shirt around the shoulder and back**; **grasp the collar and pull the shirt around the strong shoulder**; **place the strong arm into its sleeve**; and **button the shirt**. Points also depend on completing the skill **on the correct side based on the scenario’s weak side**, followed by hand hygiene and communication (30 points; 70% is 21). Tips: sit in a stable chair with armrests, lay the shirt across the lap with the inside facing up and the collar away, and use a button hook if needed.',
order('One-handed shirt dressing with right-side weakness (HOSA Skill I)',['Greet, identify and explain','Find the sleeve for the weak (right) arm','Lean forward and let the right arm hang between the legs','Slide the sleeve up the right arm with the left hand','Bring the shirt around the shoulder and back','Grasp the collar and pull it around the left shoulder','Put the left arm into the left sleeve','Button the shirt'])]],
['Using a sock aid (Skill II)',[
'A **sock aid** helps people who cannot reach their feet—after hip replacement (with hip precautions limiting bending past 90°), with back pain, obesity, arthritis or limited balance. In Skill II (5 minutes), after supplies, hand hygiene, greeting, identifying and explaining, the competitor instructs the patient to **gather the sock over the sock aid, bunched at the end**; **hold the straps and lower the sock aid to the floor**; **place the sock aid in front of the foot**; **point the foot and pull the sock aid over the toes**; and **pull the sock onto the foot and remove the sock aid**, followed by hand hygiene and communication (22 points; 70% is 15.4). Related **ADL equipment** includes the **reacher**, **long-handled shoehorn**, **elastic shoelaces**, **dressing stick**, **long-handled sponge**, **button hook**, **built-up utensils**, **plate guard**, **rocker knife** and **tub transfer bench**.',
match('Match the device to its use',[['Sock aid','Putting on socks without bending'],['Reacher','Picking up items from the floor or high shelves'],['Button hook','Fastening buttons with limited fine motor control'],['Long-handled sponge','Washing back and feet'],['Rocker knife','Cutting food with one hand'],['Tub transfer bench','Entering the tub safely while seated']]),
order('Using a sock aid (HOSA Skill II)',['Greet, identify and explain','Gather the sock over the sock aid, bunched at the end','Hold the straps and lower the sock aid to the floor','Place the sock aid in front of the foot','Point the foot and pull the sock aid over the toes','Pull the sock on and remove the sock aid'])]],
['Applying the skills',[
kase('Dressing after a stroke',{a:{text:'A client with left hemiparesis after a stroke is putting on a button-up shirt. Which arm goes into the sleeve first?',choices:[['The left (weak) arm','b','Correct. Dress the weak side first.',true],['The right (strong) arm','b','Starting with the strong arm makes the weak arm hard to reach.',false]]},b:{text:'He keeps missing the left sleeve and seems unaware of his left side. What might this be?',choices:[['Left unilateral neglect; cue him to scan to the left','c','Correct. Neglect is common after right-hemisphere strokes.',true],['Normal behavior requiring no action','c','Neglect affects safety and function.',false]]},c:{text:'With verbal cues to look left and the shirt laid out consistently, he dresses with supervision and progresses toward independence.',end:true}})]],
["Upper-extremity recovery and function by injury level",["After stroke, OT promotes motor recovery and protects the weak arm. **Positioning** supports the shoulder to prevent **subluxation** and pain—arm supported on a lap tray or pillow, never pulled by the weak arm during transfers. **Task-specific training** repeats meaningful tasks. **Constraint-induced movement therapy (CIMT)** restrains the stronger arm for much of the day while the client practices intensive tasks with the weaker arm, for clients with some active movement. **Mirror therapy** and electrical stimulation may be added. Clients with sensory loss learn to visually check the weak hand for safety near heat and sharp objects.","For spinal cord injury, expected function depends on the level. Roughly: **C1–C4**—dependent for most ADLs, uses a power wheelchair controlled by head, chin or breath; **C5**—elbow flexion, can feed with adaptive equipment; **C6**—wrist extension and **tenodesis**, can do much of self-care with equipment and may drive with adaptations; **C7–C8**—elbow extension and improving hand function, independent transfers possible; **T1 and below**—full hand function, manual wheelchair and independence in ADLs; lower lumbar levels may walk with orthoses. Skin protection with pressure relief every 15–30 minutes in a wheelchair is essential.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the injury level to typical function","pairs":[["C4","Power wheelchair controlled by head or breath"],["C5","Elbow flexion; feeding with adaptive equipment"],["C6","Wrist extension and tenodesis grasp"],["C7","Elbow extension; independent transfers possible"],["T1 and below","Full hand function; manual wheelchair"],["Pressure relief","Every 15–30 minutes when seated"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Stroke affects the opposite side; left-hemisphere strokes often cause aphasia, right-hemisphere strokes left neglect and impulsivity.','Skill I: weak arm first—lean forward, sleeve up the weak arm, shirt around the back, collar around the strong shoulder, strong arm in, button; match the scenario’s weak side.','Skill II: bunch the sock on the aid, lower by the straps, place in front of the foot, point the foot and pull, remove the aid; know reachers, button hooks and other ADL devices.'],terms:[['Hemiparesis','Weakness of one side of the body.'],['Unilateral neglect','Failure to attend to one side of space.'],['Sock aid','Device for putting on socks without bending.']],mcqs:[q('When dressing with one-sided weakness, which side is dressed first?','The weak side','The strong side','Either side','Both at once','Undress the strong side first.'),q('In Skill I, the patient lets the weak arm hang:','Between the legs while leaning forward','Behind the back','Over the head','On the armrest','Gravity helps the sleeve slide on.'),q('In Skill II, the sock is first:','Gathered over the sock aid and bunched at the end','Put directly on the foot','Turned inside out','Placed on the floor','Then the aid is lowered by its straps.'),q('A sock aid is especially useful after:','Hip replacement with hip precautions','A sprained finger','A cold','A dental cleaning','It avoids bending past 90°.'),q('Left unilateral neglect is most common after a stroke in the:','Right hemisphere','Left hemisphere','Cerebellum only','Spinal cord','The right brain attends to the left side.'),q('A tenodesis grasp is associated with a spinal cord injury at about:','C6','L5','S1','T12','Wrist extension closes the fingers.'),q('Which device helps fasten buttons?','Button hook','Sock aid','Reacher','Tub bench','It assists fine motor tasks.'),q('Energy conservation is especially important for clients with:','Multiple sclerosis','A paper cut','Seasonal allergies','Normal pregnancy only','Fatigue is a primary symptom.')],frq:{prompt:'A client with right hemiparesis after a stroke and hip precautions after a fall wants to dress independently. Describe how you would teach one-handed shirt dressing (Skill I) and sock aid use (Skill II), and name other adaptive strategies.',rubric:[['Skill I technique',['weak','right','lean forward','sleeve','collar','left arm','button']],['Skill II technique',['bunch','straps','floor','in front','point','pull']],['Adaptive equipment',['reacher','button hook','shoehorn','dressing stick','elastic','chair']],['Safety and communication',['hip precautions','90','stable chair','explain','encourage','hand hygiene']]],model:'After hand hygiene, greeting, identifying and explaining, I would seat him in a stable chair with armrests. For his shirt, I would instruct him to find the right sleeve for his weak arm, lean forward and let the right arm hang between his legs, slide the sleeve up the right arm with his left hand, move the shirt around his shoulder and back, grasp the collar and pull it around his left shoulder, put his left arm into the left sleeve and button the shirt, using a button hook if needed; undressing would start with the strong left side. Because hip precautions prevent bending past 90 degrees, I would teach the sock aid: gather the sock over the aid so it is bunched at the end, hold the straps and lower the aid to the floor, place it in front of his foot, point his foot and pull the aid over the toes, then pull the sock on and remove the aid. Other strategies include a reacher and dressing stick for pants, a long-handled shoehorn and elastic laces, laying clothes out in order and sitting for all lower-body dressing. I would speak clearly, demonstrate, encourage him and check understanding, then perform hand hygiene.'},sources:[RULES,ASA_STROKE,NINDS_SCI]};

export const O5={title:'Orthopedic, cardiopulmonary and fall-related conditions (Skills III, IV and V)',hook:'A woman with heart failure becomes breathless making her bed; a man with arthritis falls and cannot get up. OT helps clients manage effort, monitor their bodies and recover safely from falls—topics covered by Skills III, IV and V and by the physical conditions domain.',objectives:['Describe OT for fractures, arthritis, joint replacements and hand conditions.','Apply energy conservation and heart-rate monitoring for cardiopulmonary conditions (Skill V).','Teach floor-to-standing and standing-to-floor transfers (Skills III and IV).','Identify fall risks and prevention strategies.'],sections:[
['Orthopedic and hand conditions',[
'OT for **fractures** restores range of motion, strength and ADL performance after immobilization. **Osteoarthritis** wears joint cartilage; **rheumatoid arthritis** inflames joints and can deform hands; OT teaches **joint protection** (use larger, stronger joints; avoid tight sustained grips; respect pain), **energy conservation**, adaptive equipment and sometimes **orthoses (splints)**. After **total hip replacement**, OT teaches ADLs within **hip precautions** (for a posterior approach, typically no hip flexion beyond 90°, no crossing the legs and no internal rotation) using reachers, sock aids and raised toilet seats. **Total knee replacement** focuses on mobility and ADLs. **Hand therapy** addresses tendon injuries, nerve compression such as **carpal tunnel syndrome** (numbness in the thumb, index and middle fingers), and **de Quervain’s tenosynovitis**. **General deconditioning** after hospitalization is caused primarily by **reduced activity and immobility** and is treated with graded activity.',
match('Match the condition to an OT strategy',[['Rheumatoid arthritis','Joint protection and resting orthoses'],['Total hip replacement','ADLs within hip precautions with a reacher and sock aid'],['Carpal tunnel syndrome','Neutral wrist position and activity modification'],['Deconditioning after hospitalization','Graded activity to rebuild endurance'],['Hand fracture after cast removal','Range-of-motion and strengthening'],['Osteoarthritis','Energy conservation and adaptive tools']])]],
['Cardiopulmonary conditions and energy conservation',[
'Clients with **heart failure**, **coronary artery disease**, recent heart attack or **COPD** have limited endurance. OT teaches **energy conservation**: **plan** the day and alternate heavy and light tasks; **prioritize** what matters most; **pace** activities with rest breaks; **position** for efficiency by sitting to bathe, groom and cook; use **adaptive equipment**; avoid lifting and work above the shoulders; and coordinate breathing with effort (**pursed-lip** and **diaphragmatic** breathing; exhale during exertion). Activity tolerance is monitored with **heart rate**, **blood pressure**, oxygen saturation, signs such as shortness of breath, dizziness or chest pain, and the **Borg Rating of Perceived Exertion**. Activities are often graded by **metabolic equivalents (METs)**—higher METs demand more energy.',
sort('Energy-conserving or energy-wasting?',['Conserves energy','Wastes energy'],[['Sitting on a shower chair to bathe',0],['Gathering all supplies before starting',0],['Holding the breath while lifting',1],['Doing all heavy chores on one day',1],['Using a cart to move laundry',0],['Reaching overhead repeatedly',1]])]],
['Teaching heart-rate monitoring (Skill V)',[
'Clients learn to check their own pulse to stay within safe limits set by their provider. In Skill V (5 minutes), after supplies, hand hygiene, greeting, identifying and explaining, the competitor instructs the patient to **turn the hand palm up**; shows how to place **two fingers at the base of the thumb**; directs the patient to **feel the heart rate by pressing lightly in the groove below the thumb** (the radial pulse); has the patient use a **watch or clock with a second hand** to **count for one minute before beginning activity**; instructs the patient to **write down the beats per minute**; and coaches the patient to **repeat after exercise**. Hand hygiene and communication complete the skill (24 points; 70% is 16.8). Patients learn to stop and report if the rate exceeds their prescribed limit, becomes irregular or comes with chest pain, dizziness or severe shortness of breath.',
order('Teach heart-rate monitoring (HOSA Skill V)',['Greet, identify and explain','Turn the hand palm up','Place two fingers at the base of the thumb','Press lightly in the groove below the thumb','Count for one full minute before activity','Write down the beats per minute','Repeat after exercise and compare'])]],
['Floor transfers (Skills III and IV)',[
'Learning to get down to and up from the floor safely helps clients recover from falls, play with grandchildren or garden. **Standing to floor (Skill IV):** the patient places **hands on the seat of a sturdy chair**, **lowers onto the weaker knee**, **places the other knee down**, **reaches to the floor and lowers onto one hip** and then **sits back on the bottom** (26 points; 70% is 18.2). **Floor to standing (Skill III):** the patient **crawls on hands and knees to a sturdy chair**, **kneels in front of it with hands on the seat**, **lifts the stronger leg and places that foot flat on the floor** (half-kneeling) and **leans into the chair, pushing up to stand with legs and arms** (24 points; 70% is 16.8). In both skills, points depend on completing the skill **on the correct side for the scenario’s weak side**, along with hand hygiene, greeting, identifying, explaining and communication. The logic: the **weaker knee goes down first** when lowering, and the **stronger leg leads** when rising.',
order('Standing to floor (HOSA Skill IV)',['Hands on the seat of a sturdy chair','Lower onto the weaker knee','Place the other knee down','Reach to the floor and lower onto one hip','Sit back on the bottom']),
order('Floor to standing (HOSA Skill III)',['Crawl on hands and knees to a sturdy chair','Kneel facing the chair with hands on the seat','Bring the stronger leg forward, foot flat on the floor','Lean into the chair and push up with legs and arms','Stand fully and check balance'])]],
['Fall prevention',[
'Falls are a leading cause of injury in older adults. OT addresses **intrinsic** risks (weakness, poor balance, low vision, dizziness, cognitive impairment, medications, foot problems) and **extrinsic** risks (clutter, loose rugs, poor lighting, lack of grab bars, unsafe footwear). Interventions include **home safety evaluations** and modifications, balance and strength activities, teaching safe transfers and floor recovery, energy conservation, medication review referral, footwear advice and using the CDC’s **STEADI** tools. Clients who live alone may use medical alert devices. Fear of falling itself can reduce activity and increase risk, so building confidence is part of care.',
kase('Getting up after a fall',{a:{text:'A client with a weak left leg practices getting up from the floor. Which leg should come forward first to half-kneel?',choices:[['The stronger right leg','b','Correct. The stronger leg leads when rising.',true],['The weak left leg','b','The weak leg cannot push up safely.',false]]},b:{text:'Before trying to stand, what should she check?',choices:[['That she is not injured and the chair is sturdy and will not move','c','Correct. Never stand if hurt; use a stable support.',true],['That a television is on','c','Safety comes first.',false]]},c:{text:'She pushes up with both arms and the strong leg, turns and sits on the chair to rest, then reports the practice went well.',end:true}})]],
["Mobility devices, transfers and weight-bearing orders",["OT practitioners teach clients to perform occupations with mobility devices. **Canes** are held on the strong side; **walkers** are used for greater support, with a walker bag or tray to carry items (clients should never carry things in their hands while using a walker). **Wheelchairs** require locking brakes and positioning footrests before transfers. Common **transfers** include the **stand-pivot transfer** (standing and pivoting toward the strong side), the **sliding-board transfer** for clients who cannot bear weight, and **tub and toilet transfers** with benches, grab bars and raised seats. A **gait belt** is used for safety.","Orthopedic orders include **weight-bearing status**: **non-weight-bearing (NWB)**—no weight on the limb; **toe-touch** or **touch-down weight-bearing (TTWB)**—only toes touch for balance; **partial weight-bearing (PWB)**—a set percentage; **weight-bearing as tolerated (WBAT)**; and **full weight-bearing (FWB)**. OT plans ADLs that respect these orders—for example, a client who is NWB on the right leg may need a tub bench, a walker and a reacher to cook safely.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the order or device to its meaning","pairs":[["NWB","No weight on the affected limb"],["TTWB","Toes touch only for balance"],["WBAT","Weight-bearing as tolerated"],["Stand-pivot transfer","Stand and pivot toward the strong side"],["Sliding board","Transfer for clients who cannot bear weight"],["Walker bag","Carries items so hands stay on the walker"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Orthopedic OT: joint protection, hip precautions with reachers and sock aids, hand therapy; deconditioning comes mainly from reduced activity and immobility.','Energy conservation: plan, prioritize, pace, position; exhale with effort; Skill V: palm up, two fingers at the base of the thumb, count one minute before and after activity and record.','Skill IV (down): chair, weaker knee first, other knee, one hip, sit; Skill III (up): crawl to chair, kneel, stronger leg forward, push up with legs and arms.'],terms:[['Energy conservation','Strategies that reduce effort and fatigue during tasks.'],['Joint protection','Techniques that reduce stress on joints.'],['Metabolic equivalent (MET)','Unit of energy cost of an activity.']],mcqs:[q('What is the primary cause of general deconditioning in an older adult hospitalized for pneumonia?','Reduced activity level and immobility','Sleep disruption and poor nutrition','Reliance on supplemental oxygen','Overtraining','A HOSA sample question.'),q('In Skill V, the pulse is felt:','In the groove below the thumb','On the neck','On the top of the foot','Over the elbow','The radial pulse.'),q('In Skill V, the heart rate is counted for:','One minute','10 seconds','5 minutes','15 seconds','Before and after activity.'),q('When lowering from standing to the floor (Skill IV), which knee goes down first?','The weaker knee','The stronger knee','Both together','Neither—sit directly','The strong leg controls the descent.'),q('When rising from the floor (Skill III), which leg is brought forward first?','The stronger leg','The weaker leg','Neither','It does not matter','The strong leg pushes up.'),q('Which is an energy conservation strategy?','Sitting to bathe','Holding the breath during effort','Doing all chores at once','Reaching overhead repeatedly','Position for efficiency.'),q('Carpal tunnel syndrome typically causes numbness in the:','Thumb, index and middle fingers','Little finger only','Toes','Elbow','The median nerve is compressed.'),q('A common hip precaution after posterior hip replacement is:','No hip flexion beyond 90°','Sleeping on the stomach only','Crossing the legs','Deep squatting','Use a reacher and sock aid.')],frq:{prompt:'An older adult with heart failure and a weak right leg lives alone and fears falling. Describe how you would teach heart-rate monitoring (Skill V), energy conservation and floor transfers (Skills III and IV).',rubric:[['Heart-rate monitoring',['palm up','two fingers','thumb','one minute','write','repeat']],['Energy conservation',['plan','pace','rest','sit','prioritize','exhale']],['Floor transfers',['chair','weaker knee','right','stronger leg','left','push up','hip']],['Safety and fall prevention',['injur','sturdy','rug','lighting','grab bar','alert']]],model:'I would teach her to check her pulse: turn her hand palm up, place two fingers at the base of the thumb, press lightly in the groove below it and count for one full minute with a watch before activity, write the number down and repeat after activity, stopping and calling her provider if it exceeds her limit or comes with chest pain, dizziness or severe breathlessness. For energy conservation, she would plan and prioritize tasks, alternate heavy and light chores, pace herself with rest breaks, sit to bathe and cook, gather supplies first, use a cart and exhale during effort. Because her right leg is weak, to get down to the floor she would place her hands on a sturdy chair seat, lower onto her right (weaker) knee, then the left, reach down and lower onto one hip and sit. To get up, she would first make sure she is not injured, crawl to a sturdy chair, kneel with her hands on the seat, bring her stronger left leg forward with the foot flat and lean into the chair, pushing up with her legs and arms. I would also recommend removing rugs and clutter, adding lighting and grab bars and wearing a medical alert device, building her confidence with practice.'},sources:[RULES,NIAMS,AHA_HR,CDC_STEADI]};
